  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "Thanks, Ken. You're verified as an authorized user on the account. I checked the order, and the salmon roll ($9.50) was on it and wasn't refunded automatically. I can compensate you for it in one way:\n\n- **Account credit of $11.40.** That's the item price plus 20%, and it goes on the account to use on a future order.\n\nBecause you're an authorized user, compensation goes to the account as credit. I can't refund it to the card. Would you like the $11.40 credit?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Why can't it go back to the card? I'd rather have the money back on the card than credit I might not use. Is there really no way?"
  },
  {
   "role": "agent",
   "text": "I understand, but there's no way around it. When an authorized user reports a problem, any compensation has to go to the account as credit. Only the account holder, Mei, can get a refund to the card. I can't make an exception.\n\nYou have two options:\n\n1. **I issue the $11.40 credit now.** It goes on Mei's account and you can use it on any future order.\n2. **Mei contacts us herself.** She would need to verify with her own name and her email or phone number. She could then ask for a card refund of $9.50 for the salmon roll. The report window is 48 hours from delivery, and the order was delivered at 6:44 PM today.\n\nWhich would you like?"
  },
  {
   "role": "customer",
   "text": "Okay, fine, go ahead and issue the $11.40 credit. I'd rather just have it sorted tonight. Thanks for checking on it."
  },
